{:edb/db-before-t 2064 :edb/db-after-t 2065 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535377 50 #inst "2026-10-08T23:05:11.373Z" 13194139535377 true] [17592186157181 1020 #edb/ref 17592186346612 13194139535377 true] [17592186157181 1020 #edb/ref 17592186346619 13194139535377 true] [17592186157182 1020 #edb/ref 17592186346598 13194139535377 true] [17592186157182 1020 #edb/ref 17592186346626 13194139535377 true] [17592186157183 1020 #edb/ref 17592186346605 13194139535377 true] [17592186346598 1017 #edb/ref 17592186346599 13194139535377 true] [17592186346598 1089 "q-160607" 13194139535377 true] [17592186346598 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535377 true] [17592186346598 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063756/assets/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx=u(u+v),\\,y=\\frac{2u}{v+1}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is the image of the side ${S}_{1}$ under the action of $T?$" 13194139535377 true] [17592186346598 1113 #uuid "cada9cf9-93c1-5af9-b419-63eb8f69c5ea" 13194139535377 true] [17592186346598 1145 "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side ${S}_{1}.$\n\nFor the side ${S}_{1},$ we have $v=0$ and $0\\le u\\le 1.$ Substituting $v=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u(u+v),\\,\\frac{2u}{v+1}) \\\\  & =(u(u+0),\\,\\frac{2u}{0+1}) \\\\  & =({u}^{2},\\,2u).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of ${S}_{1}$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx={u}^{2},\\,y=2u,\\,0\\le u\\le 1.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)={t}^{2},\\,y(t)=2t,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nThese are parametric equations of a parabolic arc. Notice that if we eliminate $t$ from the equations, we get\n\n$$\nx={(\\frac{y}{2})}^{2}\\,⇒\\,{y}^{2}=4x\n$$\n\nwhich is a right-opening parabola whose vertex is at $(0,0).$\n\nMoreover, the endpoints of ${S}_{1}$ are mapped to the $xy$-plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & T:(0,0)\\to (0(0+0),\\,\\frac{2(0)}{0+1})=(0,0) \\\\  & T:(1,0)\\to (1(1+0),\\,\\frac{2(1)}{0+1})=(1,2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the image of ${S}_{1}$ is an arc of a right-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,0)$ to $(1,2).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063756/assets/4fc3c209e83fc1d6b4c9258cdb739fb0a73db797791cf8fcca2e51a7592d54f8.png)" 13194139535377 true] [17592186346599 1000 #edb/ref 17592186346600 13194139535377 true] [17592186346599 1000 #edb/ref 17592186346601 13194139535377 true] [17592186346599 1000 #edb/ref 17592186346602 13194139535377 true] [17592186346599 1000 #edb/ref 17592186346603 13194139535377 true] [17592186346599 1000 #edb/ref 17592186346604 13194139535377 true] [17592186346599 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535377 true] [17592186346599 1041 "selection" 13194139535377 true] [17592186346599 1104 #edb/ref 17592186346600 13194139535377 true] [17592186346599 1110 #uuid "94232b09-b342-5360-88e5-4125cbb8a3ce" 13194139535377 true] [17592186346600 1034 "An arc of a right-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,0)$ to $(1,2).$" 13194139535377 true] [17592186346600 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535377 true] [17592186346600 1111 #uuid "50d498fc-4c36-5cc7-b6d2-7c7f9e95eb1d" 13194139535377 true] [17592186346601 1034 "An arc of a right-opening parabola, traversed from $(1,2)$ to $(0,0).$" 13194139535377 true] [17592186346601 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535377 true] [17592186346601 1111 #uuid "8f36bcc2-68e8-5aab-a265-4b029f3f6cea" 13194139535377 true] [17592186346602 1034 "An arc of an upward-opening parabola, traversed from  $(1,0)$ to $(2,1).$" 13194139535377 true] [17592186346602 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535377 true] [17592186346602 1111 #uuid "cf885aed-2527-599d-ab88-09642e6d0ef4" 13194139535377 true] [17592186346603 1034 "An arc of an upward-opening parabola, traversed from $(2,1)$ to $(1,0).$" 13194139535377 true] [17592186346603 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535377 true] [17592186346603 1111 #uuid "a93c3516-a92f-53b7-bd54-905718e35b98" 13194139535377 true] [17592186346604 1034 "An arc of an upward-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,0)$ to $(1,2).$" 13194139535377 true] [17592186346604 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535377 true] [17592186346604 1111 #uuid "fdc334b4-8f69-53a1-a421-a02d8c7d9e0f" 13194139535377 true] [17592186346605 1017 #edb/ref 17592186346606 13194139535377 true] [17592186346605 1089 "q-160632" 13194139535377 true] [17592186346605 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535377 true] [17592186346605 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063756/assets/5f91cc8b30f9b764021cd84c374a328c7ffb1287a9f0dc6a211a3fadfac2fdbd.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1}\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx={u}^{2}+{v}^{2},\\,y=u.\n$$\n\nFind the image of $Δ$ under the action of $T.$ \n\n*Hint: You may assume that $T$ has no critical points in the interior of $Δ.$*" 13194139535377 true] [17592186346605 1113 #uuid "5e3c0bea-5d6e-5a25-bcb3-abdcd207f44f" 13194139535377 true] [17592186346605 1145 "We're told that $T$ has no critical points in the interior of $Δ.$ So, to map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we transform each side of $Δ$ separately:\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{1},$ where we have $v=0$ and $0\\le u\\le 1.$ Substituting $v=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =({u}^{2}+{v}^{2},\\,u) &  \\\\  & =({u}^{2}+{0}^{2},\\,u) &  \\\\  & =({u}^{2},\\,u), & 0\\le u\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a right-opening parabola traversed from the point $(0,0)$ to the point $(1,1)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{2}.$ Here, we have $u=1$ and $0\\le v\\le 1.$ Substituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =({u}^{2}+{v}^{2},\\,u) &  \\\\  & =({1}^{2}+{v}^{2},\\,1) &  \\\\  & =(1+{v}^{2},\\,1), & 0\\le v\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a horizontal line segment traversed from the point $(1,1)$ to the point $(2,1)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{3}.$ Here, we have $v=1$ and $1\\ge u\\ge 0.$ Substituting $v=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =({u}^{2}+{v}^{2},\\,u) &  \\\\  & =({u}^{2}+{1}^{2},\\,u) &  \\\\  & =({u}^{2}+1,\\,u), & 1\\ge u\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a right-opening parabola traversed from the point $(2,1)$ to the point $(1,0)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{4}.$ Here, we have $u=0$ and $1\\ge v\\ge 0.$ Substituting $u=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =({u}^{2}+{v}^{2},\\,u) &  \\\\  & =({0}^{2}+{v}^{2},\\,0) &  \\\\  & =({v}^{2},\\,0), & 1\\ge v\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nThis is a horizontal line segment traversed from the point $(1,0)$ to the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nDrawing the images of the sides of our square, we obtain the following image of $Δ:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063756/assets/56f11a4e05de6f090f94d38550b4cd21f042c1acc5d9ae67242de5fe3e9c2e50.png)" 13194139535377 true] [17592186346606 1000 #edb/ref 17592186346607 13194139535377 true] [17592186346606 1000 #edb/ref 17592186346608 13194139535377 true] [17592186346606 1000 #edb/ref 17592186346609 13194139535377 true] [17592186346606 1000 #edb/ref 17592186346610 13194139535377 true] [17592186346606 1000 #edb/ref 17592186346611 13194139535377 true] [17592186346606 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535377 true] [17592186346606 1041 "selection" 13194139535377 true] [17592186346606 1104 #edb/ref 17592186346609 13194139535377 true] [17592186346606 1110 #uuid "a71d12db-796c-5859-a298-f05a55d704aa" 13194139535377 true] [17592186346607 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063756/assets/565227489ba6ae7a657a4b2f1e0133bc117e0e666557f58f59ec0272ad8c574e.png" 13194139535377 true] [17592186346607 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139535377 true] [17592186346607 1111 #uuid "44918670-89a9-52d5-9594-e12218878a08" 13194139535377 true] [17592186346608 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063756/assets/19650cf966c3c455fa859877b4718c08aea8a343876e5817c625618098a84730.png" 13194139535377 true] [17592186346608 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139535377 true] [17592186346608 1111 #uuid "77085bba-ba65-5d40-b515-a5056a0dc853" 13194139535377 true] [17592186346609 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063756/assets/56f11a4e05de6f090f94d38550b4cd21f042c1acc5d9ae67242de5fe3e9c2e50.png" 13194139535377 true] [17592186346609 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139535377 true] [17592186346609 1111 #uuid "59307d57-6fc3-5dac-838a-11d800110a44" 13194139535377 true] [17592186346610 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063756/assets/eb6908aea50ad1c314efefdaaf922a8c637673a38f76ee8aadcbdc361f44ff78.png" 13194139535377 true] [17592186346610 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139535377 true] [17592186346610 1111 #uuid "bfa39367-aea3-54fc-85d0-f0f6a7421de5" 13194139535377 true] [17592186346611 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063756/assets/9dc7559632cbecf450389f362ca478d40576ac9646489fda281519c48f49eb16.png" 13194139535377 true] [17592186346611 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139535377 true] [17592186346611 1111 #uuid "0fabf291-2b3b-5b28-aecf-f1b826d2479a" 13194139535377 true] [17592186346612 1017 #edb/ref 17592186346613 13194139535377 true] [17592186346612 1089 "q-225035" 13194139535377 true] [17592186346612 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535377 true] [17592186346612 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063756/assets/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ and its **directed** boundary $∂Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, where $Δ$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx=v-u,\\,y={v}^{3}+u.\n$$\n\nThe image of the side ${S}_{1}$ under the action of $T$ is a **directed** line segment. Find the endpoints of this segment, stated in the order that they are traversed." 13194139535377 true] [17592186346612 1113 #uuid "1846e20c-3575-57c9-b526-6fbed45c15bf" 13194139535377 true] [17592186346612 1145 "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side ${S}_{1}.$\n\nFor the side ${S}_{1},$ we have $v=0$ and $0\\le u\\le 1.$ Substituting $v=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(v-u,\\,{v}^{3}+u) \\\\  & =(0-u,\\,{0}^{3}+u) \\\\  & =(-u,u).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of ${S}_{1}$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx=-u,\\,y=u,\\,0\\le u\\le 1.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=-t,\\,y(t)=t,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nThis is a line segment with endpoints $(0,0)$ and $(-1,1)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063756/assets/fc5daa3ee483ed2a7e78d30a247c6d4c9ae4d77691d5d5659c0b9ac2fdf6621a.png)" 13194139535377 true] [17592186346613 1000 #edb/ref 17592186346614 13194139535377 true] [17592186346613 1000 #edb/ref 17592186346615 13194139535377 true] [17592186346613 1000 #edb/ref 17592186346616 13194139535377 true] [17592186346613 1000 #edb/ref 17592186346617 13194139535377 true] [17592186346613 1000 #edb/ref 17592186346618 13194139535377 true] [17592186346613 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535377 true] [17592186346613 1041 "selection" 13194139535377 true] [17592186346613 1104 #edb/ref 17592186346615 13194139535377 true] [17592186346613 1110 #uuid "4e6c5be4-9b19-53bd-977e-e00515c3ce9b" 13194139535377 true] [17592186346614 1034 "$(-1,1)$ and $(0,0)$" 13194139535377 true] [17592186346614 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535377 true] [17592186346614 1111 #uuid "427f7dbf-8902-50af-83d8-b969e5ebab39" 13194139535377 true] [17592186346615 1034 "$(0,0)$ and $(-1,1)$" 13194139535377 true] [17592186346615 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535377 true] [17592186346615 1111 #uuid "63c0869c-b9b9-5b4f-8ea6-4ebe67232090" 13194139535377 true] [17592186346616 1034 "$(0,-1)$ and $(-1,0)$" 13194139535377 true] [17592186346616 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535377 true] [17592186346616 1111 #uuid "568537fd-afce-522c-84be-a68bc01585a0" 13194139535377 true] [17592186346617 1034 "$(0,0)$ and $(1,1)$" 13194139535377 true] [17592186346617 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535377 true] [17592186346617 1111 #uuid "70283654-127b-5a5a-afe5-735b8503fc20" 13194139535377 true] [17592186346618 1034 "$(-1,0)$ and $(0,-1)$" 13194139535377 true] [17592186346618 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535377 true] [17592186346618 1111 #uuid "add393f4-5f29-5aee-aad0-349e0dd88052" 13194139535377 true] [17592186346619 1017 #edb/ref 17592186346620 13194139535377 true] [17592186346619 1089 "q-225150" 13194139535377 true] [17592186346619 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535377 true] [17592186346619 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063756/assets/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ and its **directed** boundary $∂Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, where $Δ$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx=2v(u-1),\\,y=\\frac{1}{2}v(4-u).\n$$\n\nThe image of the side ${S}_{4}$ under the action of $T$ is a **directed** line segment. Find the endpoints of this segment, stated in the order that they are traversed." 13194139535377 true] [17592186346619 1113 #uuid "d612d8e5-c660-5c4c-a05a-812312c627df" 13194139535377 true] [17592186346619 1145 "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side ${S}_{4}.$\n\nFor the side ${S}_{4},$ we have $u=0$ and $1\\ge v\\ge 0.$ Substituting $u=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(2v(u-1),\\,\\frac{1}{2}v(4-u)) \\\\  & =(2v(0-1),\\,\\frac{1}{2}v(4-0)) \\\\  & =(-2v,2v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of ${S}_{4}$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx=-2v,\\,y=2v,\\,1\\ge v\\ge 0.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=-2t,\\,y(t)=2t,\\,1\\ge t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nThis is a line segment with endpoints $(-2,2)$ and $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063756/assets/e6ef0f52fbe6638659dd7b7f3d702d4bfbb07b83a7f7742c9b0822157908b706.png)" 13194139535377 true] [17592186346620 1000 #edb/ref 17592186346621 13194139535377 true] [17592186346620 1000 #edb/ref 17592186346622 13194139535377 true] [17592186346620 1000 #edb/ref 17592186346623 13194139535377 true] [17592186346620 1000 #edb/ref 17592186346624 13194139535377 true] [17592186346620 1000 #edb/ref 17592186346625 13194139535377 true] [17592186346620 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535377 true] [17592186346620 1041 "selection" 13194139535377 true] [17592186346620 1104 #edb/ref 17592186346625 13194139535377 true] [17592186346620 1110 #uuid "95717e6b-1a1b-5db5-974f-f282cc3ef975" 13194139535377 true] [17592186346621 1034 "$(0,2)$ and $(-2,0)$" 13194139535377 true] [17592186346621 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535377 true] [17592186346621 1111 #uuid "5416524c-9276-5c2c-b045-35d0f9cb250a" 13194139535377 true] [17592186346622 1034 "$(0,0)$ and $(2,-2)$" 13194139535377 true] [17592186346622 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535377 true] [17592186346622 1111 #uuid "b97b08c9-ad8d-5c20-9967-ad3e92b98999" 13194139535377 true] [17592186346623 1034 "$(2,-2)$ and $(0,0)$" 13194139535377 true] [17592186346623 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535377 true] [17592186346623 1111 #uuid "73b4ec85-7bf4-53ce-a8bd-e389299e3cc5" 13194139535377 true] [17592186346624 1034 "$(0,0)$ and $(-2,2)$" 13194139535377 true] [17592186346624 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535377 true] [17592186346624 1111 #uuid "dcfb67de-8999-5063-a344-07fb7ef7699c" 13194139535377 true] [17592186346625 1034 "$(-2,2)$ and $(0,0)$" 13194139535377 true] [17592186346625 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535377 true] [17592186346625 1111 #uuid "1932dd84-d621-5f7b-bc99-f09bb66eb47d" 13194139535377 true] [17592186346626 1017 #edb/ref 17592186346627 13194139535377 true] [17592186346626 1089 "q-225168" 13194139535377 true] [17592186346626 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535377 true] [17592186346626 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063756/assets/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx=u+v-2,\\,y={u}^{2}-{v}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is the image of the side ${S}_{3}$ under the action of $T?$" 13194139535377 true] [17592186346626 1113 #uuid "9087ecbd-6977-598e-9849-4ae6c5b46b16" 13194139535377 true] [17592186346626 1145 "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side ${S}_{3}.$\n\nFor the side ${S}_{3},$ we have $v=1$ and $1\\ge u\\ge 0.$ Substituting $v=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u+v-2,\\,{u}^{2}-{v}^{2}) \\\\  & =(u+1-2,\\,{u}^{2}-{1}^{2}) \\\\  & =(u-1,\\,{u}^{2}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of ${S}_{3}$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx=u-1,\\,y={u}^{2}-1,\\,1\\ge u\\ge 0.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=t-1,\\,y(t)={t}^{2}-1,\\,1\\ge t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nThese are parametric equations of a parabolic arc. Notice that if we eliminate $t$ from the equations, we get\n\n$$\ny={(x+1)}^{2}-1\n$$\n\nwhich is an upward-opening parabola whose vertex is at $(-1,-1).$\n\nMoreover, the endpoints of ${S}_{3}$ are mapped to the $xy$-plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & T:(1,1)\\to (1+1-2,\\,{1}^{2}-{1}^{2})=(0,0) \\\\  & T:(0,1)\\to (0+1-2,\\,{0}^{2}-{1}^{2})=(-1,-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the image of ${S}_{3}$ is an arc of an upward-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,0)$ to $(-1,-1).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063756/assets/b139c5b531191bc8aafbb14ad261989ef374503c63f54a567e695e287d4359be.png)" 13194139535377 true] [17592186346627 1000 #edb/ref 17592186346628 13194139535377 true] [17592186346627 1000 #edb/ref 17592186346629 13194139535377 true] [17592186346627 1000 #edb/ref 17592186346630 13194139535377 true] [1759218634662